A||||22||coolcat||Porsche brakes||||10||
Z||000000||coolcat||07-26-2001||03:43 AM||||I've been looking at these brakes for a couple of months now and I think they would be the perfect compliment to a V-8 conversion.What I was wondering is has anyone gone with this brake kit? If so how are they?  <BR> <A HREF="http://www.ultimategarage.com/bbfiero.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.ultimategarage.com/bbfiero.html</A> <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/headbang.gif">  Gettin good players is easy,gettin them to play together is the hard part<p>[This message has been edited by coolcat (edited 07-26-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000001||AusFiero||07-26-2001||08:41 AM||||I can see why no one is replying. The price is absolutely ridiculous. <P>Ok I live in Australia and that works out to around $5400 local currency for a brake conversion.  Front Only.<P>I have also talked to a local mechanical engineer who can fit my Fiero with an ABS braking system, front and rear, large cross drilled vented rotors, and much more braking than that kit for a hell of a lot less than that over the top price.<P>The ABS would be available by using a particular engine and computer I have in mind for my future full custom Fiero.<P>I would avoid it like the plague at that price. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/ausfiero1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A> | <A HREF="http://www.auto-alley.com" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Accessories</A>||202.139.20.3||reg||13
Z||000002||Adrift||07-26-2001||05:12 PM||||Aus - I'd be interested in what engine/break combo you had in mind which would allow you to have ABS.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Slammed Fiero||07-26-2001||05:30 PM||||The price is Nuts , I looked into it as well. Were doing Big Reds (biggest porsche brakes you can get) on our 911 and for all 4 it wasn't as much as the movit.de kit.<P>I opted for the 11.25" vented setup , cost me around $1400 to $1600 Canadian to do <P>JM||||reg||1
Z||000004||AusFiero||07-26-2001||07:03 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Adrift:<BR><B>Aus - I'd be interested in what engine/break combo you had in mind which would allow you to have ABS.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I am thinking of using a late model Holden Commodore 3.8 ltr engine Adrift. Same as in my family car. See <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum6/HTML/002725.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum6/HTML/002725.html</A> <P>The engine fits no probs, is a common conversion here with a kit availalble and by using the computer and modified wiring harness from the same car it then mean I can use the brakes and ABS components as well. Adapting the discs and rotors is the easiest part of the conversion of course. <P>It also would mean I would be able to maintain my car with local parts,  which are much cheaper to buy than Fiero parts.<P>Plus the cars are dropping in price now to enable it to happen. I could buy say a severely rear ended late model Commodore at the insurance auctions for around $8000 or $4000 US and use it as the parts car.  <BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/ausfiero1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A> | <A HREF="http://www.auto-alley.com" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Accessories</A>||202.139.20.3||reg||1
Z||000005||Adrift||07-26-2001||07:13 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Slammed Fiero:<BR><B><BR>I opted for the 11.25" vented setup , cost me around $1400 to $1600 Canadian to do <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>What is the difference inbetween the breaks being sold by that german company and the ones you bought for $1400-1600?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Wannabe||07-26-2001||08:48 PM||||The Held Motorsports 12" kit is not bad. I'm sure you can buy the Wilwood pieces and fabricate the custom parts for less that what they are asking, but for a complete kit, ready to bolt on and run, $2400 is not bad.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Slammed Fiero||07-26-2001||09:44 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Adrift:<BR><B> What is the difference inbetween the breaks being sold by that german company and the ones you bought for $1400-1600?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The ones I have aren't as big as the "Big Red's" that Movit.De sells. I don't know if I like the idea of that much bias with the fiero. Huge brakes on the front , stocks in the rear??<P>Mine are 11.25" all the way around , Cryo treated rotors, Camaro calipers in rear with HUGE pistons in comparison to the stock fiero , Braided lines, Wilwood calipers up front with 4 piston calipers , Polymatrix D pads , Blazer master cylinder.<P>Off the shelf parts keep things simple. From experience with 911's you don't want to replace parts. If I were going for a show car , I would do the 11.25's in back and the movit's in front. My car sees tracks and autocrosses regularly , the ability to go to autozone and get replacement parts is nice. And a lot cheaper than 911 parts.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/decisions_1.jpg"> <P>in the background our 911 , pre big reds.<P><P>------------------<BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals, White face Gauges , 70Hp Shot of NOS Autocrossed , Tracked , Dragged? (soon) abused.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Adrift||07-26-2001||09:59 PM||||Slammed - have you lost your parking brake due to the upgrade?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Slammed Fiero||07-26-2001||10:01 PM||||you can keep your parking brake , just use cadillac rear calipers , there the same as the camaro's with respect to pads , but they have the parking brake provision.<P>No I didn't keep mine , don't plan to sell the fiero anytime soon , plus I never used it<P>JM ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Adrift||07-26-2001||10:08 PM||||How much did it cost you to put together the wilwood kit, or did you buy it from a fiero store (Like Held's)?<p>[This message has been edited by Adrift (edited 07-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||artherd||07-27-2001||01:55 AM||||I love fiero owners, we all seem to be stuck in a 1984 pricing land where everything is "too expensive."  Kids, your LIFE depends directly and dearly on your brakes!!! <P>Try putting a set of brembo (any) 4-pot calipers on your brand new SUV, see how much that costs?   Price same for a BMW, Audi, or even a flipping camaro?<P>The Porsche kit is probally the best one you can buy today, the rotors are cast with the vent holes in the mould, they are not "drilled" and will therefore not crack.  The calipers are made by brembo, but they are a special prosche part, unavilable to anyone else.  Check the pic, note the aluminum hub, lowers unsprung weight (and likely improves heat transfer a tad, but that';s mainly done by porsche's turbine venting.  Take a look at a 911's rotor from the side one day.<P>I do agree that keeping the rear brakes as stock scares me a bit. not because of any lack of available torque, they have more than enough force to be effective in any conditions on the rear.  I simply do not like single pistion calipers and the high failure-rate they have.<P>I only wish the movit kit was made for the 88 cars as well (and that it used for example the 996 calipers and rotors on the rear.)<P>As it is, my only choice is the Ryane/Held 13" setup, which is priced lower, but the component's quality can't quite be said to equal Porsche (brakes are one of a few things they do *totally* right in my opinioin)<P>Still, it's not a bad setup, and I'd LOVE to hear from anyone with either the 12 or 13" Held/Ryane brakes!!<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000012||Will||07-27-2001||09:12 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B>I do agree that keeping the rear brakes as stock scares me a bit. not because of any lack of available torque, they have more than enough force to be effective in any conditions on the rear.  I simply do not like single pistion calipers and the high failure-rate they have.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>High failure rate?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><BR>If they had a failure rate that was high enough to worry aobut they wouldn't be used in everything. Car manufacturers don't like to be sued.<BR>Track use is a horse of a different color, however.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Slammed Fiero||07-27-2001||10:38 AM||||Mine is not a kit , you can buy it from archie as a kit , but I have a few different things.<P>The Wilwoods were ordered straight from wilwood, I researched sizes , emailed them and they suggested what I needed. As for single piston caliper failure rates, what exactly are you talking about, I don't think you know.<P>As for Movit's Kit , it IS too expensive ,I suggest you order up a automotion catalogue off of the net , or pick up a copy of excellence.<P>Lots of places sell Big red kits (same components as movit's kit) for 911's a front kit runs $1600-$1700. Your telling me it costs another $1000 to adapt it to a fiero. I don't think so (we adapted 911 brakes to our corvair for less than that). We paid $3400 for all 4 calipers and rotors (big red) that went on our 911.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.nascarplace.com/brembo/itm03188.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.nascarplace.com/brembo/itm03188.htm</A> <P><BR>I tried to put together a more cost effective race worthy system, I would put my 11.25" system against any fiero that had a Movit kit in front and stocks in rear.<P>Porsche brakes are great. Huge brakes are great, but if your just some joe wannabe a kit like the porsche brakes for a fiero is a waste , they will never get to operating temp on a sunday drive. The difference between a porsche's brakes and other cars is that there designed for longevity. <P>I have had a lot of experience with driving fast 911's takes a few hard laps to get the brakes to work. But if you wanna spend $3000 on lookin pretty go ahead. Lots of people want a cheaper alternative , thats whay I got a fiero , It's a very universal car.<P>JM||||reg||1
Z||000014||Tina||07-27-2001||10:59 AM||||First time I saw them on a Porsche, all I could think is I want  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>But the $,was a mayor deterrent.<BR>Even if my sis in germany would of bought them for me,(little less $) I still would of had to buy bigger rims to make them fit.<BR>(17 inch requirement, if I remember right)<P>Tina  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||HarryG||07-27-2001||12:08 PM||||Slammed: Have you checked your brakes with a G-tech?<P><BR> ||||reg||5
Z||000016||artherd||07-27-2001||09:25 PM||||I am speaking of my three fieros, ALL of which have had MORE THAN ONE caliper sieze.<BR>(on it's slider pins.)  Perhaps they are quite reliable on other cars, but on the fiero it is my expierence that single piston calipers are failure prone in the extreme.<P>It simply seems a poor design to my mind, although it may save the carmakers $100 (or even $500) it is of little comfort when you lock (or even just sieze and cause a distortion in brake liniarity from driver to passenger side..) a caliper at 70mph. or 120mph...<P>Jonothan, you are correct, the purpose of brakes this large and powerful is *repeated* stoping from very high speeds.  What would be considered "abuse" to stock brakes. going from 130 to 40 to 100 on a racetrack. Rinse, repeat.<P><BR>$1,000 is a tad steep for two brackets plus machine time. But I'd rather they not fail. Ever.  (Still, I would probally buy the off-the-shelf compoennts, and fashion my own adapters, I have access to a good machine shop. But not everybody does.)<P>How do you handle the bias between your 4-pot front willwoods, and the rear single pistons? (more to the point, are the areas relatively similar, and if so, what proportioning valve do you use, is it ajustable, and how do you like it ? :)<P>I do disagree with you on one point, I do not think a passanger car's brakes can be good enough. If your 11.25 or Ryane's 12" setup came on every fiero from the factory, I would applaud it.  (Though I would like ABS on my fiero, on any road car I drive infact.  With defeat for the track of course! :)  Vacumm assist needs to die as well, either unassisted, or a system like BMW's hydraulic needs to replace this antiquated throwback.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000017||Will||07-27-2001||11:56 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B>I am speaking of my three fieros, ALL of which have had MORE THAN ONE caliper sieze.<BR>(on it's slider pins.)  Perhaps they are quite reliable on other cars, but on the fiero it is my expierence that single piston calipers are failure prone in the extreme.<P>It simply seems a poor design to my mind, although it may save the carmakers $100 (or even $500) it is of little comfort when you lock (or even just sieze and cause a distortion in brake liniarity from driver to passenger side..) a caliper at 70mph. or 120mph...<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>They don't seize when properly maintained. Anything breaks if you abuse it. Lack of proper maintenance <I>is</I> abuse.<BR>Mine are all running on the original sliders on the original grease and still move freely by hand.<P>Floating calipers aren't just a cost issue:<BR>They're lighter than fixed calipers. And that's unsprung weight.<BR>They give the same clamping force as fixed piston calipers with lower master cylinder volume requirements.<BR>An they're just as effective. If there were a marked advantage to have fixed calipers, the C5 Corvette would have fixed calipers. However, the C5 has two piston floaters front and single piston floaters rear.||||reg||1
Z||000018||artherd||07-28-2001||03:39 AM||||In what fasion could one abuse the calipers in a fiero to cause failure? (riding the brakes for a few hundred miles, and associated radical overtemping is <BR>about all I can think of...)<P>What could have been done to these poor <BR>cars before I goy my paws on them"?! :)<P>The C5 has in it's design constraints, the word cost.   If cost were no object, it would be mid-engined :) <P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000019||Galen||07-28-2001||03:54 AM||||Read all of what he said, not just what you wanna see.. "<I>Lack of proper maintenance is abuse</I>"  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR><FONT COLOR="purple">Ray</FONT> <FONT COLOR="gray">-</FONT> <FONT COLOR="purple">"And God said there shall be one in thy owns image, and he made me"</FONT><BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ray19nc/download.html" TARGET=_blank>Download FieroProject.com Chat Client v1.50 Today</A>||||reg||1
Z||000020||artherd||07-30-2001||12:00 AM||||uh. I was not aware that slider pins and caliper slides were "mantinence" items?<P>Or "wear" items of any sort.<P>What do you mean, what causes these failures?<P>(other than slashing the boots, all of mine were gas tight btw, had grease, and still locked!)<P>Seriously, I want to know.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000021||AusFiero||07-30-2001||01:56 PM||||Ok personally I think this is better value for brakes. Not much more than the FRONT ONLY Porsche brake kit. <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/bigbrakes.jpg"> <P>This kit includes.<BR>4 13" cross-drilled, slotted and gold zinc plated rotors (Australian Made)<BR>8 brake pads <BR>4 aluminum four piston calipers <BR>4 SS braided hoses with fittings <BR>2 parking brake calipers <BR>1 high volume master cylinder <BR>2 rear caliper mounting brackets <BR>1 proportioning valve with adapters <BR>All hardware and instructions.<P>Kits are available for 84 to 87 Fieros and a 88 kit is also available. Your emergency brake is also retained with this kit. You will need to run 17" wheels to fit the 13" rotors.<P>Contact me by email if you want to know more. <BR>||||reg||14
Z||000022||Slammed Fiero||07-30-2001||02:16 PM||||Once again I built my kit to be easily replaceable and effective. If I wanted it for show I would have bought a complete kit.<P>As for the wilwoods , I use a Tilton Brake bias setup. I have gotten stock brakes so hot that you can't touch the wheel and there is smoke coming out. <P>Everyone has their own needs / wants / ect.  Complete kits are nice for ease of install , and I imagine work amazing. I was trying to stay within a budget , once I broke the budget i was just trying to get it all together. Keep in mind what the Canadian Dollar currenlty sits at in relation to brake kit costs.<P>JM||||reg||1
